        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Three Principles of the People</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">1905 CE – present</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Thinkers</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-yat-sen">Sun Yat-sen</a>, who set out the Principles; Hu Hanmin, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-jingwei">Wang Jingwei</a> and Feng Ziyou, who fleshed them out in Minbao; Zhu Zhixin, who defended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tongmenghui">Tongmenghui</a>'s “state socialism”; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-kai-shek">Chiang Kai-shek</a>, who interpreted them as party leader; Dai Jitao, whose conservative reading the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a> followed; Liang Qichao, the reformist rival whose ideas on the nation and the general will left their mark</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Key texts</span><span class="info-stat-val">the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tongmenghui">Tongmenghui</a> manifesto (1905); Sun's article in the first issue of Minbao (1905); The International Development of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>; The Fundamentals of National Reconstruction (1924); Sun's lectures, published as The Three Principles of the People (English translation by Frank Price, 1927); Sun's political testament (1925, drafted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-jingwei">Wang Jingwei</a>)</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Core ideas</span><span class="info-stat-val">nationalism, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s revival from imperial domination; democracy, with the four powers of the people (suffrage, recall, initiative, referendum) and a five-branch government; people's livelihood, covering clothing, food, housing and mobility; land equalisation by self-assessment; a distinction between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> of the people and the ability of the government; political tutelage before constitutional government; the Confucian ideal of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/datong">Datong</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Today</span><span class="info-stat-val">the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a> made the Principles the official ideology of its state, and after its government's retreat to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-kai-shek">Chiang Kai-shek</a> added texts on education and leisure; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a> honours Sun, less so the Principles, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deng-xiaoping">Deng Xiaoping</a>'s era linked his economic plans to the Four Modernisations; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a>'s People's Power Party, founded in 2015, took them as its guiding ideology</span></div>
