    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-yat-sen">Sun Yat-sen</a> first set out the Principles of nationalism, democracy and people's welfare in 1905, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tongmenghui">Tongmenghui</a>, formed in Tokyo that year, put them in its manifesto.</li><li>Sun's democracy rested on a distinction between the people's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> and the government's ability, a government of five branches and a period of political tutelage before constitutional rule.</li><li>After Sun's death in 1925 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a> made the Principles, as Sun gave them in his 1924 lectures and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-kai-shek">Chiang Kai-shek</a> interpreted them, the official ideology of its state.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The name “Three Principles of the People” comes from Feng Ziyou, who later claimed that while editing a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tongmenghui">Tongmenghui</a> paper he shortened the phrase “promoting nationalism, democracy, and people's welfare” into the term.</p></aside>
    </section>

